      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $3x-y=4$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x-y &=4 \\\\\n-y &=-3x+4 \\\\\ny &=3x-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the slope is $3$.\n\nOnly $y=3x+2$ has slope $3$. The other choices have slopes $-3$, $\\frac{1}{3}$, $1$, and $4$. Its $y$-intercept is $2$, different from $-4$, so it is a distinct parallel line.\n\nTherefore, choose $y=3x+2$.",
