      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is parallel to $2x+3y=6$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Parallel lines have the same slope. First convert the given line to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+3y &=6 \\\\\n3y &=-2x+6 \\\\\ny &=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required slope is $-\\frac{2}{3}$. The choices are also in standard form, so isolate $y$ to compare their slopes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x+2y=9 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\\n4x+6y=-6 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1 \\\\\n2x-3y=6 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\\n2x+6y=6 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+1 \\\\\n3x-2y=9 &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $4x+6y=-6$ has slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$. Its $y$-intercept is $-1$, different from $2$, so it is a distinct parallel line.\n\nTherefore, choose $4x+6y=-6$.",
